1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,676
In this video, we take a React app from a laptop to the internet,

2
00:00:04,676 --> 00:00:09,366
on Amazon EKS, the way a security-minded DevOps team would do it at work.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:12,839
Terraform builds the whole platform on AWS:

4
00:00:12,839 --> 00:00:16,940
a private network, a Kubernetes cluster and a load balancer.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:20,494
Seventy two resources, in twelve and a half minutes.

6
00:00:21,033 --> 00:00:25,281
Then every git push builds the app, scans it for vulnerabilities,

7
00:00:25,281 --> 00:00:29,333
signs it and deploys it, with no AWS password stored anywhere.

8
00:00:29,867 --> 00:00:31,677
Then I attack my own cluster.

9
00:00:31,677 --> 00:00:35,484
Privileged containers, stealing the node's cloud credentials,

10
00:00:35,484 --> 00:00:39,977
moving sideways between apps, and dropping requests during a deployment.

11
00:00:39,977 --> 00:00:44,283
Two of those attacks found real weaknesses, and I fix them on camera.

12
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:49,419
And at the end, I delete everything and prove that nothing is left.

13
00:00:49,419 --> 00:00:52,773
The whole thing ran on AWS for sixty two minutes.

14
00:00:52,773 --> 00:00:53,868
Let us build it.

15
00:00:55,633 --> 00:00:56,899
Here is the plan.

16
00:00:56,899 --> 00:01:00,698
First the architecture, so you see the big picture.

17
00:01:01,233 --> 00:01:03,935
Then the infrastructure with Terraform:

18
00:01:03,935 --> 00:01:09,338
the VPC network, the EKS cluster, the container registry, and the permissions.

19
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,356
Then the application:

20
00:01:11,356 --> 00:01:14,476
React, a secure Docker image, Trivy scanning,

21
00:01:14,476 --> 00:01:19,885
the GitHub Actions pipeline, and how it gets into AWS without any stored keys.

22
00:01:20,433 --> 00:01:24,662
Then exposing it to the internet with the AWS Load Balancer Controller,

23
00:01:24,662 --> 00:01:28,475
the Kubernetes security settings, and my attacks with the fixes.

24
00:01:29,033 --> 00:01:32,609
And finally, how to delete everything safely and prove it.

25
00:01:32,609 --> 00:01:36,740
All the code is linked in the description, so you can follow along.

26
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:39,916
Why does this matter?

27
00:01:39,916 --> 00:01:44,030
Getting a container running on Kubernetes takes an afternoon.

28
00:01:44,030 --> 00:01:46,998
Getting it running safely is the actual job.

29
00:01:46,998 --> 00:01:49,156
Here is what usually goes wrong.

30
00:01:49,156 --> 00:01:49,561
First:

31
00:01:49,561 --> 00:01:55,833
long lived AWS keys stored in the CI system, one of the most common causes of cloud breaches.

32
00:01:56,367 --> 00:01:56,863
Second:

33
00:01:56,863 --> 00:01:58,424
known vulnerabilities.

34
00:01:58,424 --> 00:02:04,526
Base images often ship with security bugs that already have a fix, and nobody notices.

35
00:02:05,067 --> 00:02:05,478
Third:

36
00:02:05,478 --> 00:02:10,420
worker nodes with public IP addresses, sitting directly on the internet.

37
00:02:10,967 --> 00:02:11,420
Fourth:

38
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:16,216
containers running as root, or able to steal the node's cloud credentials.

39
00:02:16,767 --> 00:02:17,411
And fifth:

40
00:02:17,411 --> 00:02:22,626
the test cluster that someone forgot to delete, quietly costing money every hour.

41
00:02:23,167 --> 00:02:29,276
This project fixes every one of these, and for each fix there is a test or a command you can rerun yourself.

42
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:33,561
Here is the whole platform on one page.

43
00:02:33,561 --> 00:02:36,478
It starts with a git push to the main branch.

44
00:02:37,033 --> 00:02:43,218
GitHub Actions runs the checks, and on main it builds, scans, signs and deploys the app.

45
00:02:43,767 --> 00:02:48,298
To reach AWS, the pipeline uses OIDC to get a short lived role.

46
00:02:48,298 --> 00:02:55,851
That role can push to exactly one container repository, and deploy into exactly one Kubernetes namespace.

47
00:02:55,851 --> 00:02:56,786
Nothing else.

48
00:02:57,333 --> 00:03:03,905
Everything lives in one AWS region, Frankfurt, in a dedicated VPC that spans two availability zones.

49
00:03:03,905 --> 00:03:08,308
The public subnets hold only the load balancer and the NAT gateway.

50
00:03:08,867 --> 00:03:13,697
The EKS worker nodes live in private subnets, with no public IP addresses.

51
00:03:13,697 --> 00:03:20,421
They run our two app pods, one in each zone, plus the load balancer controller and the cluster add-ons.

52
00:03:20,967 --> 00:03:29,066
Images are stored in Amazon ECR, and the EKS control plane encrypts secrets with a dedicated KMS key and writes audit logs.

53
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,703
Tool number one is Terraform.

54
00:03:32,703 --> 00:03:38,213
Instead of clicking through the AWS console, we describe the infrastructure as code.

55
00:03:38,213 --> 00:03:41,034
That makes it reviewable in a pull request,

56
00:03:41,034 --> 00:03:46,480
repeatable for every student, and, just as important, destroyable with one command.

57
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:49,293
Every resource gets the same tags:

58
00:03:49,293 --> 00:03:52,018
project, owner, and managed by Terraform.

59
00:03:52,018 --> 00:03:56,538
At the end, we use these tags to prove that nothing was left behind.

60
00:03:57,100 --> 00:04:01,054
Then two guard rails, because I built this in a company account.

61
00:04:01,054 --> 00:04:03,710
The region variable only accepts Frankfurt,

62
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:08,900
and allowed account IDs makes Terraform refuse to run against any other AWS account.

63
00:04:09,433 --> 00:04:11,096
All versions are pinned:

64
00:04:11,096 --> 00:04:14,977
the AWS provider, and the community VPC and EKS modules.

65
00:04:14,977 --> 00:04:21,283
The modules are well tested and widely used, so we write less code and make fewer mistakes.

66
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:24,709
Tool two is the network:

67
00:04:24,709 --> 00:04:28,619
a VPC with the address range 10.42 dot 0 dot 0 slash 16,

68
00:04:28,619 --> 00:04:34,763
spread over two availability zones, so the app survives the loss of a whole data center.

69
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:38,755
Private subnets hold the worker nodes and the pods.

70
00:04:38,755 --> 00:04:41,668
Public subnets hold only the load balancer.

71
00:04:41,668 --> 00:04:44,106
Nodes never get a public IP address.

72
00:04:44,667 --> 00:04:47,571
A NAT gateway lets the private nodes reach out,

73
00:04:47,571 --> 00:04:51,959
for example to pull images, while nothing on the internet can reach in.

74
00:04:51,959 --> 00:04:53,689
One NAT keeps the lab cheap.

75
00:04:53,689 --> 00:04:56,223
In production you would use one per zone.

76
00:04:56,767 --> 00:05:04,021
These two subnet tags let the AWS Load Balancer Controller discover where to place internet-facing and internal load balancers.

77
00:05:04,567 --> 00:05:06,010
And two security habits:

78
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:08,535
the default security group allows nothing,

79
00:05:08,535 --> 00:05:13,766
and VPC flow logs record every connection, which you will want during an investigation.

80
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,878
Tool three is Amazon EKS, managed Kubernetes.

81
00:05:18,878 --> 00:05:26,535
We use version 1.37, the newest one in standard support, and place the cluster in the private subnets.

82
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:31,447
The control plane writes audit logs, so every API call is recorded,

83
00:05:31,447 --> 00:05:35,470
and Kubernetes secrets are encrypted with a dedicated KMS key.

84
00:05:36,033 --> 00:05:43,274
Authentication mode API means we use EKS access entries to map AWS identities to Kubernetes permissions.

85
00:05:43,274 --> 00:05:46,895
No more editing the old aws-auth config map by hand.

86
00:05:47,433 --> 00:05:51,469
The VPC CNI add-on gets network policy enforcement switched on,

87
00:05:51,469 --> 00:05:56,210
and the Pod Identity agent lets pods get AWS permissions without any keys.

88
00:05:56,767 --> 00:06:00,707
Two t3 medium nodes, encrypted disks, and an important one:

89
00:06:00,707 --> 00:06:04,715
instance metadata version two only, with a hop limit of one.

90
00:06:04,715 --> 00:06:05,650
Remember this.

91
00:06:05,650 --> 00:06:11,661
Later, I try to steal the node's credentials from a pod, and this setting is why it fails.

92
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:14,755
Now let us build it.

93
00:06:14,755 --> 00:06:16,246
First, terraform plan.

94
00:06:16,246 --> 00:06:18,956
Read the last line before anything else:

95
00:06:18,956 --> 00:06:22,614
seventy three to add, zero to change, zero to destroy.

96
00:06:22,614 --> 00:06:31,490
In a shared company account, that zero to change and zero to destroy is your proof that you will not touch anyone else's resources.

97
00:06:32,033 --> 00:06:33,412
Then terraform apply.

98
00:06:33,412 --> 00:06:36,301
The NAT gateway took one and a half minutes.

99
00:06:36,301 --> 00:06:38,861
The EKS control plane is the slow part:

100
00:06:38,861 --> 00:06:41,291
eight minutes and forty four seconds.

101
00:06:41,291 --> 00:06:46,543
The nodes took two minutes, and the load balancer controller twenty one seconds.

102
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:50,425
Seventy two resources in twelve and a half minutes.

103
00:06:50,425 --> 00:06:56,227
Seventy two, not seventy three, because I switched off one module feature we do not need,

104
00:06:56,227 --> 00:06:59,878
an extra identity provider for the older IRSA mechanism.

105
00:06:59,878 --> 00:07:03,920
Fewer global IAM objects in a shared account is always better.

106
00:07:05,667 --> 00:07:07,160
kubectl get nodes:

107
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:09,981
two nodes, Ready, Kubernetes 1.37.

108
00:07:09,981 --> 00:07:11,973
Look at the last column:

109
00:07:11,973 --> 00:07:13,466
external IP, none.

110
00:07:13,466 --> 00:07:17,283
The nodes are not reachable from the internet.

111
00:07:17,283 --> 00:07:21,100
And their internal IPs, 10.42.14 and 10.42.30,

112
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:26,576
are in two different subnets, so two different availability zones.

113
00:07:27,133 --> 00:07:29,282
The cluster settings confirm it:

114
00:07:29,282 --> 00:07:32,974
access entries, audit logs, secrets encrypted with KMS.

115
00:07:32,974 --> 00:07:39,620
The API endpoint is public, and that is a deliberate trade-off I will explain in the pipeline part.

116
00:07:40,167 --> 00:07:45,219
And the system pods are running, including two replicas of the load balancer controller,

117
00:07:45,219 --> 00:07:47,286
which Terraform installed with Helm.

118
00:07:49,033 --> 00:07:51,008
Tool four is the application.

119
00:07:51,008 --> 00:07:55,229
It is my profile card, rebuilt as a small React app with Vite.

120
00:07:55,229 --> 00:08:00,608
It has five unit tests, a linter, and an npm audit for vulnerable dependencies.

121
00:08:01,167 --> 00:08:03,232
The Dockerfile has two stages.

122
00:08:03,232 --> 00:08:06,399
Stage one uses Node to build the static files.

123
00:08:06,399 --> 00:08:12,733
Stage two only copies the result, so Node.js and all the build tools never reach production.

124
00:08:13,267 --> 00:08:19,563
Both base images are pinned by digest, so nobody can swap them for different content behind the same tag.

125
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:22,483
The runtime is nginx unprivileged:

126
00:08:22,483 --> 00:08:28,510
it runs as a normal user on port 8080, and in Kubernetes its file system is read-only.

127
00:08:29,067 --> 00:08:32,592
nginx also adds strict security headers to every response,

128
00:08:32,592 --> 00:08:37,152
like a content security policy that only allows our own scripts and styles.

129
00:08:37,152 --> 00:08:41,103
You will see them later, coming back from the real load balancer.

130
00:08:42,867 --> 00:08:45,686
Tool five is Trivy, a free security scanner.

131
00:08:45,686 --> 00:08:49,018
And it caught a real problem in my very first image.

132
00:08:49,018 --> 00:08:52,862
The official nginx base image, pinned to the latest version,

133
00:08:52,862 --> 00:08:57,219
contained a library called pcre2 with a high severity vulnerability.

134
00:08:57,219 --> 00:08:59,270
A fixed version already existed.

135
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:04,588
The gate returned exit code one, so in the pipeline nothing would have been pushed.

136
00:09:05,133 --> 00:09:06,557
The fix is three lines:

137
00:09:06,557 --> 00:09:13,243
switch to root for one command, apply the Alpine security updates, and switch back to the unprivileged user.

138
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:14,560
Scan again:

139
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:17,116
zero vulnerabilities, exit code zero.

140
00:09:17,116 --> 00:09:19,257
Notice the flag ignore unfixed:

141
00:09:19,257 --> 00:09:25,475
we block on problems we can actually fix, so the gate stays strict without becoming noise.

142
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:29,221
Tool six is GitHub Actions.

143
00:09:29,221 --> 00:09:34,226
The CI workflow runs five jobs on every pull request and every push.

144
00:09:34,226 --> 00:09:36,213
None of them can touch AWS.

145
00:09:36,767 --> 00:09:41,718
First, gitleaks scans the entire git history for leaked keys and passwords.

146
00:09:41,718 --> 00:09:47,528
Second, the app job runs the linter, the unit tests, the dependency audit and the build.

147
00:09:48,067 --> 00:09:51,551
Third, the container job lints the Dockerfile with hadolint,

148
00:09:51,551 --> 00:09:54,686
builds the image and runs the Trivy gate you just saw.

149
00:09:55,233 --> 00:09:59,043
Fourth, the infrastructure job formats and validates the Terraform,

150
00:09:59,043 --> 00:10:02,852
scans Terraform and the Kubernetes manifests for misconfigurations,

151
00:10:02,852 --> 00:10:07,002
and checks the manifests against the Kubernetes schemas with kubeconform.

152
00:10:07,567 --> 00:10:09,783
And fifth, the pipeline checks itself:

153
00:10:09,783 --> 00:10:13,340
every third party action must be pinned to a full commit SHA,

154
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:16,956
and actionlint and zizmor look for workflow security mistakes.

155
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:23,342
When Trivy scanned my Terraform, it reported three critical findings.

156
00:10:23,342 --> 00:10:24,621
And they were real:

157
00:10:24,621 --> 00:10:31,214
the EKS API endpoint is public, open to any IP address, and the nodes can connect out to anywhere.

158
00:10:31,767 --> 00:10:33,678
I kept all three, on purpose.

159
00:10:33,678 --> 00:10:40,469
GitHub's hosted runners have no fixed IP addresses, so they need the public endpoint, protected by IAM.

160
00:10:40,469 --> 00:10:45,809
The production fix is a private endpoint with self-hosted runners inside the VPC.

161
00:10:45,809 --> 00:10:47,061
The important part:

162
00:10:47,061 --> 00:10:51,346
each exception is written down, with the reason and the real fix.

163
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:53,567
And each exception expires.

164
00:10:53,567 --> 00:10:57,890
On that date the pipeline fails again, and somebody has to look at it.

165
00:10:57,890 --> 00:11:02,829
A security exception without an owner and an end date quietly becomes permanent.

166
00:11:03,367 --> 00:11:05,713
The scanner also caught me once more.

167
00:11:05,713 --> 00:11:10,026
In the CI test I rendered the manifests with a placeholder registry,

168
00:11:10,026 --> 00:11:14,339
registry dot example, and Trivy refused it as an untrusted registry.

169
00:11:14,339 --> 00:11:21,062
In the real deployment the image comes from Amazon ECR, so I changed the test to use an ECR-style address.

170
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:27,183
Tool seven is the most important security idea in the pipeline:

171
00:11:27,183 --> 00:11:27,531
OIDC.

172
00:11:27,531 --> 00:11:30,661
The pipeline stores no AWS access key at all.

173
00:11:30,661 --> 00:11:34,070
Each job asks GitHub for a signed identity token,

174
00:11:34,070 --> 00:11:39,148
and AWS exchanges it for temporary credentials that expire after the job.

175
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:44,555
This account already had a GitHub identity provider, used by other teams.

176
00:11:44,555 --> 00:11:47,614
So Terraform only reads it with a data source.

177
00:11:47,614 --> 00:11:50,606
It never changes it, and it never deletes it.

178
00:11:51,167 --> 00:11:52,799
The trust policy is strict:

179
00:11:52,799 --> 00:11:57,576
the token must be meant for AWS, and its subject must be this exact repository,

180
00:11:57,576 --> 00:12:02,655
running in its protected production environment, which only accepts the main branch.

181
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:05,445
And here is real failure number one.

182
00:12:05,445 --> 00:12:07,191
My first deploy failed with:

183
00:12:07,191 --> 00:12:10,184
not authorized to assume role with web identity.

184
00:12:10,184 --> 00:12:10,870
The reason:

185
00:12:10,870 --> 00:12:17,355
new GitHub repositories now use an immutable subject that includes the numeric owner and repository IDs.

186
00:12:17,355 --> 00:12:19,288
That is a security improvement:

187
00:12:19,288 --> 00:12:25,648
if someone deletes this repository and creates a new one with the same name, it cannot assume my role.

188
00:12:25,648 --> 00:12:28,517
One Terraform change, and the next run worked.

189
00:12:30,267 --> 00:12:32,550
Tool eight is the deploy workflow.

190
00:12:32,550 --> 00:12:36,647
It runs on every push to main, and it is split into two jobs.

191
00:12:36,647 --> 00:12:41,146
Job one builds the image and scans it, with no cloud access at all.

192
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:47,224
Only if job one passed does job two start, inside the protected production environment.

193
00:12:47,224 --> 00:12:50,208
It gets temporary AWS credentials through OIDC,

194
00:12:50,208 --> 00:12:55,287
pushes the image to ECR by digest, waits for ECR's own scan as a second opinion,

195
00:12:55,287 --> 00:12:58,779
signs the image with cosign, deploys that exact digest,

196
00:12:58,779 --> 00:13:04,239
checks its own permissions, and finally tests the live site through the load balancer.

197
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:05,620
Why two jobs?

198
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:08,962
The scanning tools never run next to AWS credentials,

199
00:13:08,962 --> 00:13:15,899
a vulnerable image never even reaches the registry, and the deploy can only ship exactly what job one scanned.

200
00:13:17,633 --> 00:13:20,364
Here is a real run, triggered by a push.

201
00:13:20,364 --> 00:13:21,388
Build and scan:

202
00:13:21,388 --> 00:13:22,822
thirty three seconds.

203
00:13:22,822 --> 00:13:23,778
Deploy to EKS:

204
00:13:23,778 --> 00:13:26,168
two minutes and fifty four seconds.

205
00:13:26,168 --> 00:13:30,333
Three minutes and thirty seven seconds from git push to live,

206
00:13:30,333 --> 00:13:33,815
and GitHub attaches the live URL to the deployment.

207
00:13:34,367 --> 00:13:38,419
Getting here took two more real fixes, and both are lessons.

208
00:13:38,419 --> 00:13:42,877
ECR's scan result is not available for a few seconds after a push,

209
00:13:42,877 --> 00:13:46,929
and the standard AWS CLI waiter gives up instead of waiting.

210
00:13:46,929 --> 00:13:54,156
And when an image has zero findings, ECR returns nothing at all, which my first check treated as a failure.

211
00:13:54,156 --> 00:13:55,440
Pipelines are code.

212
00:13:55,440 --> 00:13:56,993
They need testing, too.

213
00:13:58,733 --> 00:14:00,174
Inside the deploy job:

214
00:14:00,174 --> 00:14:06,263
the ECR scan completed with zero findings, and the cosign signature was created and verified.

215
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:09,535
kubectl applies the service account, service,

216
00:14:09,535 --> 00:14:15,674
deployment, disruption budget, ingress and network policies, and waits until the rollout is complete.

217
00:14:16,233 --> 00:14:19,347
And then the pipeline proves its own limits.

218
00:14:19,347 --> 00:14:20,692
kubectl auth can-i:

219
00:14:20,692 --> 00:14:24,371
yes, it may create deployments in its own namespace.

220
00:14:24,371 --> 00:14:27,980
But it may not touch kube-system, not read secrets,

221
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:32,509
not grant itself cluster permissions, and not delete namespaces.

222
00:14:32,509 --> 00:14:35,128
If any answer changes, the job fails.

223
00:14:36,867 --> 00:14:39,635
Tool nine exposes the app to the internet.

224
00:14:39,635 --> 00:14:44,052
The AWS Load Balancer Controller, installed by Terraform with Helm,

225
00:14:44,052 --> 00:14:48,271
watches for Ingress objects and creates real AWS load balancers.

226
00:14:48,271 --> 00:14:54,665
It gets its AWS permissions through EKS Pod Identity, so there are no keys in the cluster either.

227
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:58,967
Our Ingress asks for an internet facing Application Load Balancer,

228
00:14:58,967 --> 00:15:01,821
which the controller places in the public subnets.

229
00:15:02,367 --> 00:15:06,165
Target type IP sends traffic straight to the pod IP addresses,

230
00:15:06,165 --> 00:15:11,618
health checks use the healthz endpoint, and invalid HTTP headers are dropped at the edge.

231
00:15:12,167 --> 00:15:13,704
One honest limitation:

232
00:15:13,704 --> 00:15:18,665
I have no domain name for this lab, and a TLS certificate needs a name.

233
00:15:18,665 --> 00:15:20,831
So the site runs on plain HTTP.

234
00:15:20,831 --> 00:15:26,770
With a domain you add a certificate from AWS Certificate Manager and two annotations,

235
00:15:26,770 --> 00:15:29,076
and the load balancer does HTTPS.

236
00:15:30,833 --> 00:15:32,275
And here it is, live.

237
00:15:32,275 --> 00:15:35,502
The Ingress got a public load balancer address.

238
00:15:35,502 --> 00:15:38,386
The deployment runs an exact image digest,

239
00:15:38,386 --> 00:15:43,398
never a tag, and the two pods run on different nodes, in different zones.

240
00:15:43,933 --> 00:15:45,949
curl through the load balancer:

241
00:15:45,949 --> 00:15:50,761
HTTP 200, with the content security policy and the other security headers,

242
00:15:50,761 --> 00:15:54,143
coming all the way from nginx in the private subnet.

243
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:56,853
In the browser:

244
00:15:56,853 --> 00:15:59,459
the profile card, served from Amazon EKS.

245
00:15:59,459 --> 00:16:02,509
The footer shows the version and the git commit,

246
00:16:02,509 --> 00:16:07,974
which the pipeline bakes into every build, so you always know exactly what is running.

247
00:16:09,733 --> 00:16:11,636
Tool ten is Kubernetes itself.

248
00:16:11,636 --> 00:16:17,723
The pod does not get a service account token, because the app never talks to the Kubernetes API.

249
00:16:18,267 --> 00:16:21,147
It runs as a non-root user with a high user ID,

250
00:16:21,147 --> 00:16:25,681
and with the default seccomp profile, which blocks dangerous system calls.

251
00:16:26,233 --> 00:16:28,781
The container cannot escalate privileges,

252
00:16:28,781 --> 00:16:33,067
its file system is read-only, and all Linux capabilities are dropped.

253
00:16:33,067 --> 00:16:36,298
The only writable place is a small temporary folder.

254
00:16:36,833 --> 00:16:40,065
CPU and memory limits keep it from starving its neighbours,

255
00:16:40,065 --> 00:16:44,448
and the readiness probe means traffic only reaches a pod that is actually ready.

256
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,985
And the namespace itself, created by Terraform,

257
00:16:47,985 --> 00:16:52,050
enforces the Kubernetes Pod Security standard called restricted.

258
00:16:52,050 --> 00:16:54,654
Let us see what that does to an attacker.

259
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:57,395
Time to attack.

260
00:16:57,395 --> 00:17:01,772
First, I try to start a privileged container in the app namespace,

261
00:17:01,772 --> 00:17:05,221
which would give it almost full control of the node.

262
00:17:05,221 --> 00:17:08,934
Refused by Pod Security, with a list of five violations.

263
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:14,700
Second, I pretend an attacker got into the app pod and tries to download a tool.

264
00:17:14,700 --> 00:17:17,105
It cannot even resolve a domain name:

265
00:17:17,105 --> 00:17:21,979
the default deny network policy blocks all outgoing traffic, including DNS.

266
00:17:22,533 --> 00:17:24,723
Third, the classic cloud attack:

267
00:17:24,723 --> 00:17:29,993
from a pod, ask the instance metadata service for the node's AWS credentials.

268
00:17:29,993 --> 00:17:35,536
The token request times out, because of the hop limit of one we set in Terraform.

269
00:17:35,536 --> 00:17:39,915
And the old version one interface, without a token, answers 401.

270
00:17:39,915 --> 00:17:42,653
The node's credentials are out of reach.

271
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:46,206
The fourth attack worked.

272
00:17:46,206 --> 00:17:53,791
My first network policy allowed port 8080 from the whole VPC, because the load balancer lives in the VPC.

273
00:17:53,791 --> 00:17:58,703
From a pod in a different namespace, I could reach the app directly:

274
00:17:58,703 --> 00:17:59,353
HTTP 200.

275
00:17:59,900 --> 00:18:03,778
The reason is an EKS detail that catches many people.

276
00:18:03,778 --> 00:18:08,095
With the VPC CNI, pod IP addresses come from the VPC range.

277
00:18:08,095 --> 00:18:12,411
So allow the VPC also means allow every pod in the cluster.

278
00:18:12,967 --> 00:18:13,464
The fix:

279
00:18:13,464 --> 00:18:18,744
only allow the two public subnets, where the load balancer's network interfaces live.

280
00:18:18,744 --> 00:18:20,173
I tested it live first:

281
00:18:20,173 --> 00:18:23,838
the pods stayed ready and the load balancer kept answering.

282
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:26,871
And the lateral attack now times out.

283
00:18:26,871 --> 00:18:30,344
The fix went into git, and the pipeline deployed it.

284
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:35,969
The fifth attack was not an attacker, it was my own deployment.

285
00:18:35,969 --> 00:18:40,881
I requested the site twice a second while the pipeline rolled out a new version.

286
00:18:40,881 --> 00:18:44,074
Three requests out of two hundred and eighty failed:

287
00:18:44,074 --> 00:18:46,531
one bad gateway error, and two timeouts.

288
00:18:46,531 --> 00:18:50,153
Small, but in production that is real users getting errors.

289
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:51,324
The cause:

290
00:18:51,324 --> 00:18:55,945
a pod was stopped before the load balancer had stopped sending it traffic.

291
00:18:55,945 --> 00:18:57,444
The fix has three parts.

292
00:18:57,444 --> 00:19:03,501
Pod readiness gates, so a new pod only counts as ready when the load balancer says it is healthy.

293
00:19:03,501 --> 00:19:07,997
A pre-stop pause of fifteen seconds, so a terminating pod keeps serving.

294
00:19:07,997 --> 00:19:13,054
And a load balancer deregistration delay of ten seconds, shorter than that pause.

295
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:15,580
Same test, after the fix:

296
00:19:15,580 --> 00:19:20,569
four hundred and one requests, four hundred and one successful.

297
00:19:20,569 --> 00:19:22,469
Zero downtime, measured.

298
00:19:23,033 --> 00:19:25,029
One more thing the test revealed:

299
00:19:25,029 --> 00:19:29,865
for about twenty eight seconds, the old and the new version served side by side.

300
00:19:29,865 --> 00:19:34,642
For a single page app that can mean an old page asks a new pod for an old file.

301
00:19:34,642 --> 00:19:39,600
Teams solve that by keeping old assets for a while, or by serving them from a CDN.

302
00:19:41,333 --> 00:19:44,078
And now, the part that most tutorials skip:

303
00:19:44,078 --> 00:19:45,354
deleting everything.

304
00:19:45,354 --> 00:19:46,503
The order matters.

305
00:19:46,503 --> 00:19:49,567
The load balancer was created by the controller,

306
00:19:49,567 --> 00:19:53,141
not by Terraform, so if you run terraform destroy first,

307
00:19:53,141 --> 00:19:58,630
the load balancer and its security groups are left behind, and deleting the VPC fails.

308
00:19:58,630 --> 00:20:03,928
So the script deletes the Ingress first, and waits until the load balancer is gone.

309
00:20:04,467 --> 00:20:05,951
Then terraform destroy:

310
00:20:05,951 --> 00:20:08,016
seventy two resources destroyed.

311
00:20:08,016 --> 00:20:12,986
The cluster took three and a half minutes, the NAT gateway one minute twenty.

312
00:20:13,533 --> 00:20:15,875
And then a separate script proves it:

313
00:20:15,875 --> 00:20:19,165
every resource type, by name and by tag, is checked.

314
00:20:19,165 --> 00:20:24,608
Everything is gone, and the shared GitHub identity provider is still there, untouched.

315
00:20:24,608 --> 00:20:26,759
Two more lessons from this script.

316
00:20:26,759 --> 00:20:33,594
The AWS tagging index still lists deleted resources for a while, so the script checks each one's real state.

317
00:20:33,594 --> 00:20:39,289
And the encryption key waits seven days before AWS really deletes it, which costs nothing.

318
00:20:41,033 --> 00:20:42,340
What did this cost?

319
00:20:42,340 --> 00:20:47,911
At on-demand prices in Frankfurt, this setup costs roughly thirty cents per hour:

320
00:20:47,911 --> 00:20:53,345
the EKS control plane, two small nodes, the NAT gateway, and the load balancer.

321
00:20:53,900 --> 00:20:59,292
From the first terraform apply to the verified teardown, it ran for sixty two minutes.

322
00:20:59,292 --> 00:21:01,737
That is an estimated thirty five cents.

323
00:21:01,737 --> 00:21:07,254
Delete your lab when you are done, and Kubernetes on AWS is a very cheap thing to learn.

324
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:11,394
What would I add for real production?

325
00:21:11,394 --> 00:21:14,047
HTTPS with your domain and a certificate,

326
00:21:14,047 --> 00:21:19,029
and AWS WAF on the load balancer to block common attacks and abusive traffic.

327
00:21:19,567 --> 00:21:23,529
A private API endpoint with self-hosted runners inside the VPC,

328
00:21:23,529 --> 00:21:27,931
which removes two of the accepted risks, and one NAT gateway per zone.

329
00:21:28,467 --> 00:21:32,851
Terraform state in S3 with locking, so a team can work on it safely,

330
00:21:32,851 --> 00:21:39,234
and signature verification at admission, so the cluster only runs images that this pipeline signed.

331
00:21:39,234 --> 00:21:42,651
My previous project shows exactly that, with Kyverno.

332
00:21:43,200 --> 00:21:48,984
And autoscaling with the horizontal pod autoscaler and Karpenter, plus monitoring and alerts.

333
00:21:50,733 --> 00:21:52,863
Want to build it yourself?

334
00:21:52,863 --> 00:22:00,808
You need an AWS account, Terraform, the AWS CLI and kubectl, and your own fork of the repository.

335
00:22:01,367 --> 00:22:03,676
Change two variables to your fork:

336
00:22:03,676 --> 00:22:07,005
the repository name, and the OIDC subject prefix.

337
00:22:07,005 --> 00:22:10,333
The command to read your prefix is in the README.

338
00:22:10,867 --> 00:22:15,320
Set your account ID as the allowed account, and run terraform apply.

339
00:22:15,320 --> 00:22:17,416
It takes about thirteen minutes.

340
00:22:17,967 --> 00:22:23,062
Put the deploy role from the Terraform output into a GitHub secret in the production environment,

341
00:22:23,062 --> 00:22:24,848
and switch on the deploy variable.

342
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:29,023
Push a commit to main and watch the pipeline deploy it.

343
00:22:29,023 --> 00:22:31,064
The run shows you the live URL.

344
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:33,972
And please, delete it the same day:

345
00:22:33,972 --> 00:22:37,902
run the teardown script, and read the verification output.

346
00:22:37,902 --> 00:22:39,934
Then your bill stays at cents.

347
00:22:41,700 --> 00:22:43,127
That is the project.

348
00:22:43,127 --> 00:22:48,265
Seventy two AWS resources as code, a pipeline with no stored cloud keys,

349
00:22:48,265 --> 00:22:51,190
scanning at every step, and five attacks:

350
00:22:51,190 --> 00:22:55,899
three refused, and two that found real weaknesses, which we fixed.

351
00:22:56,433 --> 00:22:59,576
Most of what you learned here comes from the failures:

352
00:22:59,576 --> 00:23:01,962
the scanner finding a real vulnerability,

353
00:23:01,962 --> 00:23:06,501
the new GitHub subject format, the lateral movement, and the dropped requests.

354
00:23:06,501 --> 00:23:08,886
That is what real DevOps work looks like.

355
00:23:09,433 --> 00:23:13,293
The code and the documentation are linked in the description.

356
00:23:13,293 --> 00:23:15,318
Which attack would you try next?

357
00:23:15,318 --> 00:23:19,431
Tell me in the comments, and subscribe for the next real project.

358
00:23:19,431 --> 00:23:20,696
Thanks for watching.
